<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What types of conveyors are used in bakeries?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Bakeries use cooling spiral conveyors, oven band conveyors, proofing conveyors, depositor infeed belts, pan conveyors, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line conveyors</a>. Each production stage requires different belt materials, temperature ratings, and speed controls.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a cooling spiral conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A cooling spiral conveyor moves baked products through a helical path inside a tower, providing extended <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling</a> time in a small floor footprint. Stacking the belt in tiers gives a long cooling time in a fraction of the floor space a straight-line cooler needs. Spirals can be ambient, forced-air, or refrigerated.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What belt materials work for bakery conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Wire mesh belts (stainless for ovens, carbon steel for cooling), modular plastic (acetal or polypropylene for ambient handling), polyurethane (FDA-compliant direct food contact), and silicone (non-stick for sticky doughs). Selection depends on temperature, product type, and food contact requirements.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How do bakery conveyors meet food safety standards?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Bakery conveyors meet food safety through BISSC certification to ANSI/BISSC/Z50.2, FDA-compliant contact materials, open-frame construction for cleaning access, and tool-free belt removal. BISSC standards address cleanability, material selection, and hygienic design specific to baking equipment. See <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyor standards</a> for more detail.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What is a proofing conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A proofing conveyor transports dough through a temperature- and humidity-controlled enclosure where yeast fermentation occurs. The bakery sets temperature, humidity, and dwell time for each dough formula, and the conveyor runs slowly enough to deliver that dwell time. Spiral proofers save floor space; straight-line proofers suit smaller operations.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-6">What belt is used on a proofing conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-6" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Proofing conveyor belts are usually modular plastic (polypropylene or acetal) or stainless steel wire mesh, chosen to resist warm, humid conditions and allow COP cleaning. When dough rides directly on the belt, the belt is a food-contact surface and must be FDA-compliant and release dough cleanly. Spiral proofers need a spiral-rated belt.</p>

</div>
</div>
